<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The play flavor's half of SPEC.md §3.4's split: the restricted permissions
  and network access kept out of the main manifest and declared here instead.
  DeclaredPermissionsTest pins the split in both directions.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Firebase Analytics merges `AD_ID` into whatever includes it, and Play's
      Advertising ID declaration is answered "not used"
      (docs/play-store-declarations.md). Removing it keeps that answer true:
      Analytics then reports against the per-install app-instance ID, which is
      specific to this app, cannot be joined to activity elsewhere, and resets
      on a clear-data or reinstall.

      What this costs is the ads-adjacent surface — audience export to Google
      Ads, and the demographics that are inferred from the advertising ID —
      none of which Snoozemo has any use for. `DeclaredPermissionsTest` was
      written to fail the moment Analytics arrived, precisely so this was
      decided rather than inherited; this is that decision.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      The Geofencing API needs the background grant to deliver an exit while
      the app holds no foreground service, and this flavor deliberately has
      none. Declared with the maintainer's approval (2026-08-22); the Play
      Console declaration it commits us to is SPEC.md §3.5's risk.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The snooze service runs in the foreground while something is actually
      watching for a departure (SPEC.md §6.10). Not for the sake of the
      notification — the ongoing card is posted either way — but for the
      process: an ordinary started service is destroyed routinely, and a device
      log showed the presence watch closing 67 s after arming with nothing
      looking for the next hour, then the geofence exit arriving to a *refused*
      background service start. A foreground service is what keeps the
      collection alive to receive what the fence already delivers.

      The `location` type, chosen by the maintainer (2026-09-08) with the
      policy text in front of them: Android's own page for this type carries an
      "Alternatives" line reading "if your app needs to be triggered when the
      user reaches specific locations, consider using the geofence API
      instead", which is SPEC.md §3's April 2026 concern still live in Google's
      current wording. Snoozemo does both — the fence delivers the exit, the
      service is what survives to hear it — and this is the type that describes
      what the process is doing while it waits.

      `play`-only, like the background grant above and for the same reason. The
      gate in the service is the tracking mode, not the build: a duration-only
      snooze starts no watch and needs no service to outlive one, so it takes
      none.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      Repeated from the main manifest, where the location family is declared
      and where it stays. It is a no-op at merge — the same
      permission twice is one permission — and it is here so the check that ties
      a `location` service to its prerequisites can see them together:
      `ForegroundServicePermission` reads the manifest the `<service>` is
      declared in rather than the merged one, so with the type here and the
      grant only over there it reports the type as unbacked. Better a stated
      duplicate than a suppressed check.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      Crash reporting and Firebase Analytics use this, and nothing else
      (SPEC.md §12). Both sit behind one consent the user has to give first,
      so the permission is held by a build that sends nothing until asked.

      It was absent until Crashlytics landed, and the day it
      landed the Play Data Safety answer moved off "no data collected, no data
      shared" onto crash logs, diagnostics and the installation identifier
      Crashlytics records; Analytics later added app interactions and its own
      app-instance identifier — docs/play-store-declarations.md carries the
      answers and docs/PRIVACY.md the user-facing statement.

      The floor of SPEC.md §12 is unchanged by this permission — no
      coordinates, no SSID or BSSID, no user-typed place name goes anywhere,
      and the on-device debug log still leaves the phone only when the user
      shares it. What Crashlytics sends is a stack trace; what Analytics sends
      is the events the SDK collects on its own. The user can turn both off in
      Settings, with one switch, and neither sends anything until they say yes.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application>
        <!--
          The type is declared here rather than beside the service in the main
          manifest, so a build without these foreground-service permissions
          ships a plain started service: a manifest type it can never use would
          be a claim the build does not make.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="app.snoozemo.snooze.SnoozeService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="location"
            tools:node="merge" />

        <!--
          Off until the app says otherwise. Crashlytics would otherwise start
          collecting during process creation — before Snoozemo has read the
          stored opt-out — so an install where the user has turned reporting
          off would still upload the first crash after every launch. The gate
          in CrashReporter applies the stored choice at startup; both SDK
          switches persist what they are given, so later launches honor the
          last-known choice even before the store has been read.

          The cost of this ordering is the narrow window it leaves: a crash
          between process creation and the gate applying `true` is not
          reported. That is the right way round — an unreported crash is a
          missing diagnostic, a wrongly-reported one breaks a promise made in
          Settings.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_crashlytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <!--
            Analytics needs its own switch, and the reasoning above is why
            rather than a second copy of it: Firebase initializes every SDK
            from the same ContentProvider, so an SDK without a default here
            decides for itself before any of Snoozemo's code has run. Analytics
            is the one where that bites hardest — it records its own automatic
            events with no call site of ours to gate, so an install that has
            not answered the question yet would already have sent `first_open`
            and `session_start` by the time it is asked.

            The advertising ID is switched off separately below; the permission
            itself is removed in the main manifest, so the identifier that
            remains is per-install and cannot be joined to activity in other
            apps.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_analytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_adid_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>
